{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2023",
    "lib": ["ES2023"],
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    "rootDir": "src",
    "outDir": "dist",

    "strict": true,
    /*
      Dead code is not free here, because `verbatimModuleSyntax` below keeps import
      statements exactly as written: an import nobody reads is still emitted, still resolved
      at startup, and still shipped. Four of them were — `node:crypto`, `createReadStream`,
      `pipeline` and `node:events`, left behind when the checksum machinery moved into the
      reader — along with a `rowsIn` helper that had a doc comment and no callers.

      `noUnusedParameters` was declined here for years on the grounds that "the codebase names
      a deliberately unused argument `unused` rather than `_unused`, which that check does not
      accept, and renaming twenty callbacks to satisfy it would be the tail wagging the dog".
      Neither half was true. There is exactly one such argument in `src`, in `editDistance`,
      and it is already spelled `_unused` — the spelling the check does accept. So the reason
      not to turn it on had stopped existing, and turning it on found two: `estimateOutput`
      kept taking `recordDuration` and `recordStarts` after `rowsInRange` was lifted out of it
      and the caller started passing them there instead. `noUnusedLocals` cannot see a
      parameter, so they sat in the signature being computed and handed over for nothing.
    */
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,

    "declaration": true,
    /*
      Off, because a declaration map can only point at source the package does not ship.

      `files` is ["dist", ...] on purpose, and `inlineSources` puts the TypeScript inside the
      .js.map so a stack trace resolves in an installed copy. TypeScript has no equivalent for
      declaration maps: every .d.ts.map shipped said `"sources": ["../src/index.ts"]` with no
      `sourcesContent`, so "Go to Definition" in a consumer's editor followed it to a path
      that is not there. Without the map the same jump lands in the .d.ts, which is accurate
      and present.
    */
    "declarationMap": false,
    "sourceMap": true,
    // Sources embedded in the maps, because src/ is not in the published package and a map
    // pointing at ../src/cli.ts resolves to nothing once installed. A stack frame inside
    // edf2csv should land on the line that produced it, not on a missing file.
    "inlineSources": true,
    "removeComments": false,
    "newLine": "lf",

    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "test"]
}
